      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225200",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Let $a$ and $b$ be integers and $n$ be a positive integer. If $a$ and $n$ are coprime, and $x_{1} \u2208 N$ is a solution to the linear congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$, then this solution is unique up to congruence modulo $n$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: We are given that $gcd(a, n) = 1$ and $x_{1}$ is a solution of $ax\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L2$: So, $ax_{1}\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L3$: Assume for a contradiction that there exists $x_{2} \u2208 N$ such that $x_{1}\u2262x_{2}(modn)$ and $ax_{2}\u2261b(modn)$.\n> $L4$: Then, $a(x_{1} - x_{2})\u22610(modn)$.\n> $L5$: Therefore, $x_{1} - x_{2}\u22610(modn)$.\n> $L6$: Finally, $x_{1}\u2261x_{2}(modn)$, but this is a contradiction.\n> $L7$: Thus, the solution to the linear congruence $ax\u2261b(modn)$ is unique up to congruence modulo $n$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from lines $L2$ and $L3$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line $L4$ by the fact that {{field-3}} and applying the {{field-4}} property of congruences."
